{
 "title": "Safe wrappers",
 "blocks": [
  {
   "t": "p",
   "text": "The best practice in professional systems programming is to confine `unsafe` code inside small functions and offer the outside world a **completely safe** API. The caller never has to write `unsafe`, and invalid inputs are filtered out in the wrapper."
  },
  {
   "t": "code",
   "code": [
    "extern \"stdlib.h\" fn rand() -> i32",
    "extern \"stdlib.h\" fn srand(seed: u32)",
    "",
    "fn get_c_random(max: i32) -> i32:",
    "    if max <= 0:",
    "        return 0",
    "    unsafe:",
    "        raw = rand()",
    "        return raw % max",
    "",
    "fn main():",
    "    dice = get_c_random(6) + 1",
    "    print(\"dice:\", dice)"
   ],
   "run": false,
   "local": true
  },
  {
   "t": "p",
   "text": "In the example below C's `sqrt` is hidden inside a safe function that returns a `Result`:"
  },
  {
   "t": "code",
   "code": [
    "extern \"math.h\" fn sqrt(x: f64) -> f64",
    "",
    "fn square_root(x: f64) -> Result[f64, str]:",
    "    if x < 0.0:",
    "        return Err(\"negative number\")",
    "    unsafe:",
    "        return Ok(sqrt(x))",
    "",
    "fn main():",
    "    match square_root(16.0):",
    "        Ok(v):",
    "            print(v)",
    "        Err(m):",
    "            print(m)"
   ],
   "run": false,
   "local": true
  },
  {
   "t": "quiz",
   "id": "c13-l04-q1",
   "q": "What is the purpose of a safe wrapper?",
   "options": [
    "To slow the code down",
    "To gather `unsafe` code in one small place and keep the caller safe",
    "To remove `extern`"
   ],
   "answer": 1,
   "why": "The `unsafe` surface to audit shrinks."
  }
 ]
}
